</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:306px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">TARI Kibaha (Pwani)</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:437px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">Sugarcane</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:306px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">TARI Mikocheni (Dar es Salaam)</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:437px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">Coconut, Biotechnology</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:306px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">TARI Hombolo (Dodoma)</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:437px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">Sorghum and millets</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:306px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">TARI Makutupora (Dodoma)</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:16px; vertical-align:top; width:437px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">Grapes</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:1px solid black; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:306px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">TARI Kihinga (Kigoma)</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"background-color:#ffffff; border-bottom:1px solid black; border-left:none; border-right:1px solid black; border-top:none; height:17px; vertical-align:top; width:437px\">\r\n			<p style=\"text-align:justify !important\"><span style=\"font-size:15px !important\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Open Sans&quot;,sans-serif !important\"><span style=\"color:#212121 !important\">Oil palm</span></span></span></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>','who-we-are',1,'2025-06-09 13:16:36','2025-06-28 04:08:12'),(100,'Research Labs','Research Labs',1,'<p>RESEARCH LAB</p>','<p>RESEARCH LAB</p>','research-labs',1,'2025-06-12 05:14:47','2025-06-12 05:14:47'),(103,'Varieties Developed','Varieties Developed',0,'<p>Varieties%20Developed</p>','<p>Varieties%20Developed</p>','varieties-20developed-2',1,'2025-06-12 05:50:01','2025-06-12 06:45:50'),(104,'PLANT PROTECTION','PLANT PROTECTION',1,'<h1 style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>PLANT PROTECTION</strong></h1>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Hydrophilic polymers have been prepared by grafting synthetic monomers on starch, a naturally occuring polysaccharide polymer for improving the water absorption and delaying its loss. Some polymers based on carboxymethyl cellulose developed recently have shown nearly 33000% m/m water absorption. These hydrogels can be used in diverse areas of agriculture such as dryland/rainfed agriculture, hi-tech horticulture and floriculture, nursery raising in soil less media, soil reclamation, agroforestry, artificial lawns and landscapes, terrace gardening etc. Synthetic pesticides, neem emulsifiable concentrate (EW), Azadirachtin-A concentrate, neem oil microemulsion/microemulsion forming concentrate and processes of making dihydroazadirachtin-A concentrate under ambient conditions have been developed A process for preparing an herbicidal composition comprising of neem leaves extract for the control of Phalaris minor weed without affecting the growth of wheat has also been developed Natural and synthetic polymers, either alone or in combination with inorganic inert have been successfully developed as seed coats. Such coats have been developed employing botanical active ingredients ex. neem meliacins etc. Seed coats based on botanical pesticide will help in improving germination, viability, plantability of a seed or vigour of an agronomic plant that is grown from a treated seed that is planted in a pest infested location, by preventing the fungal/nematode/insect infestation that is a pest for the agronomic plant and against which a meliacin compound has bioactivity Neem oil coated urea (NOCU), Pusaneem GOlden urea and Neem bitter coated urea has been developed. Field efficacy established on rice showed yield increase from 7-17% Dillapoile, an undesirable constituent present on the tune of 20-40% in the essential oil of Anethum sowa ROxb., and its dihydro derivative prepared in laboratory have been developed as indigenous substitutes for the imported and internationally recognized synergist piperonyl butoxide (PBO) Controlled release formulations of phorate have been successfully developed through its monolithic dispersions in polystyrene, polyvinyl chloride, polymethyl-methacrylate, polyethylene glycol and cellulose acetate, ethyl cellulose and starch xanthate. These formulations have been found to provide comparable pest control at one half to one fourth of the phorate content as compared to Thimet 10G, its commercial product A technology fo rmosquito larvicidal preparations based on Rabdosea melissoides has been developed. The essential oil of r. melissoides and its phenolic and non-phenolic constitutents have been developed as potential mosquito larvicidal products. Novel pesticidal oxime esters, alkane diol/polyol mono/di/poly alkanoates, 4-methyl-6-pentyl-a-pyrones have been developed as potent wide spectrum pesticidal products. The compounds are effective particularly as nematicides against Meloidogyne incognita and Rotylenchulus reniformis and fungicides against polyphagous fungi Rhizoctonis solani and Sclerotium rolfsi Cost effective and indigenous know-how for conventional pesticides namely thiophanate-methyl and mancozeb have been developed and passed to NRDC for commercialization.</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Entomology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Cole Crops</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Okra</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Brinjal</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Tomato</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Cucurbits</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect control in Soybean</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect control in Mustard</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Management of insect pests of Pulses</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect control in Cotton</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Management of insect pests in storage grains</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Rice</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Nematology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">The following technologies have been identified for commercialization</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>A novel bio-pesticidal formulation based on an indigenous heat tolerant species of entomopathogenic nematode,<em>&nbsp;Steinernema thermophilum</em>,</strong>&nbsp;incorporated and immobilized into water insoluble superabsorbent hydrogel matrix, has been developed for the first time by a simple method that comprises swelling of the xerogel in appropriate volume of aqueous suspension of the nematode. The formulation obtained has improved shelf life ranging from few hours to 36 months at storage temperatures verying from 5 to 50<sup>0</sup>C. The formulation is not infected by any microorganism and hence does not require any anti-microbial or anti-fungal chemicals</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Application technology for using entomopathogenic nematodes against foliar pest:&nbsp;<strong>Follow spray of 2000 infective juveniles of&nbsp;<em>Steinernema thermophilum</em>&nbsp;per ml liquid formulation, has been found useful for managing diamond backmoth (<em>Plutella xylostella</em>) on cabbage, cotton boll worm (<em>Spodoptera litura</em>) on cotton and whitefly (<em>Bemisia tabaci</em>) on brinjal and tobacco</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><em><strong>Photorhabdus luminescens</strong></em><strong>&nbsp;formulation for insect control</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><em><strong>Heterorhabditis indica</strong></em><strong>&nbsp;- phagostimulant based alginate beads:</strong>&nbsp;The entomopathogenic nematode&nbsp;<em>Heterorhabditis indica</em>&nbsp;(IARI strain) is a highly virulent nematode with the ability to kill insect pests belonging to several orders of economic importance. The infective stage juveniles of the nematodes are embedded in sodium alginate beads along with an insect phagostimulant. The bead can either be broadcast in the soil or applied as spot treatment. Laboratory trials have shown the mortality of&nbsp;<em>Spodoptera litura</em>,&nbsp;<em>Helicoverpa</em>&nbsp;spp.,&nbsp;<em>Holotrichia</em>&nbsp;spp., within 48 hours. The shelf-life of the beads is 6-8 months</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Neem seed treatment for nematode management</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Control of Ear-cockle and tundu disease of wheat.</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Plant Pathology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Diagnostic kit for graft transmissible pathogens in citrus:&nbsp;</strong>A cost effective and user friendly diagnostic kit for graft transmissible pathogens in citrus has been developed which includes an inexpensive simplified unified nucleic acid (NA) template preparation for both RNA and DNA pathogens and uniform PCR conditions. Uniform PCR conditions coupled with unified NA template preparation will not only reduce the cost of detection but also reduce the time and energy particularly in view of the fact that citrus is hosts of multiple infections of graft transmissible pathogens. The kit also provides information on important viruses and greening bacterium affecting citrus in India</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Biocontrol Technology for Crop Growth Promotion and Disease Management:</strong>&nbsp;Biological management is an important alternative supporting system of crop protection in which microorganisms have been widely exploited for managing fungal, bacterial, viral and nematodal disease. This technology is an important component of Integrated Pest Management and Organic Farming System. Fungal bioagents&nbsp;<em>Aspergillus niger&nbsp;</em>and&nbsp;<em>Trichoderma harzianum</em>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<em>Trichoderma viridae</em>&nbsp;have been utilized to develop bio-formulations.</p>','<h1 style=\"text-align:center\"><strong>PLANT PROTECTION</strong></h1>\r\n\r\n<p >Hydrophilic polymers have been prepared by grafting synthetic monomers on starch, a naturally occuring polysaccharide polymer for improving the water absorption and delaying its loss. Some polymers based on carboxymethyl cellulose developed recently have shown nearly 33000% m/m water absorption. These hydrogels can be used in diverse areas of agriculture such as dryland/rainfed agriculture, hi-tech horticulture and floriculture, nursery raising in soil less media, soil reclamation, agroforestry, artificial lawns and landscapes, terrace gardening etc. Synthetic pesticides, neem emulsifiable concentrate (EW), Azadirachtin-A concentrate, neem oil microemulsion/microemulsion forming concentrate and processes of making dihydroazadirachtin-A concentrate under ambient conditions have been developed A process for preparing an herbicidal composition comprising of neem leaves extract for the control of Phalaris minor weed without affecting the growth of wheat has also been developed Natural and synthetic polymers, either alone or in combination with inorganic inert have been successfully developed as seed coats. Such coats have been developed employing botanical active ingredients ex. neem meliacins etc. Seed coats based on botanical pesticide will help in improving germination, viability, plantability of a seed or vigour of an agronomic plant that is grown from a treated seed that is planted in a pest infested location, by preventing the fungal/nematode/insect infestation that is a pest for the agronomic plant and against which a meliacin compound has bioactivity Neem oil coated urea (NOCU), Pusaneem GOlden urea and Neem bitter coated urea has been developed. Field efficacy established on rice showed yield increase from 7-17% Dillapoile, an undesirable constituent present on the tune of 20-40% in the essential oil of Anethum sowa ROxb., and its dihydro derivative prepared in laboratory have been developed as indigenous substitutes for the imported and internationally recognized synergist piperonyl butoxide (PBO) Controlled release formulations of phorate have been successfully developed through its monolithic dispersions in polystyrene, polyvinyl chloride, polymethyl-methacrylate, polyethylene glycol and cellulose acetate, ethyl cellulose and starch xanthate. These formulations have been found to provide comparable pest control at one half to one fourth of the phorate content as compared to Thimet 10G, its commercial product A technology fo rmosquito larvicidal preparations based on Rabdosea melissoides has been developed. The essential oil of r. melissoides and its phenolic and non-phenolic constitutents have been developed as potential mosquito larvicidal products. Novel pesticidal oxime esters, alkane diol/polyol mono/di/poly alkanoates, 4-methyl-6-pentyl-a-pyrones have been developed as potent wide spectrum pesticidal products. The compounds are effective particularly as nematicides against Meloidogyne incognita and Rotylenchulus reniformis and fungicides against polyphagous fungi Rhizoctonis solani and Sclerotium rolfsi Cost effective and indigenous know-how for conventional pesticides namely thiophanate-methyl and mancozeb have been developed and passed to NRDC for commercialization.</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Entomology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Cole Crops</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Okra</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Brinjal</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Tomato</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Cucurbits</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect control in Soybean</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect control in Mustard</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Management of insect pests of Pulses</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect control in Cotton</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Management of insect pests in storage grains</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Insect Pest Management in Rice</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Nematology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">The following technologies have been identified for commercialization</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>A novel bio-pesticidal formulation based on an indigenous heat tolerant species of entomopathogenic nematode,<em>&nbsp;Steinernema thermophilum</em>,</strong>&nbsp;incorporated and immobilized into water insoluble superabsorbent hydrogel matrix, has been developed for the first time by a simple method that comprises swelling of the xerogel in appropriate volume of aqueous suspension of the nematode. The formulation obtained has improved shelf life ranging from few hours to 36 months at storage temperatures verying from 5 to 50<sup>0</sup>C. The formulation is not infected by any microorganism and hence does not require any anti-microbial or anti-fungal chemicals</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Application technology for using entomopathogenic nematodes against foliar pest:&nbsp;<strong>Follow spray of 2000 infective juveniles of&nbsp;<em>Steinernema thermophilum</em>&nbsp;per ml liquid formulation, has been found useful for managing diamond backmoth (<em>Plutella xylostella</em>) on cabbage, cotton boll worm (<em>Spodoptera litura</em>) on cotton and whitefly (<em>Bemisia tabaci</em>) on brinjal and tobacco</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><em><strong>Photorhabdus luminescens</strong></em><strong>&nbsp;formulation for insect control</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><em><strong>Heterorhabditis indica</strong></em><strong>&nbsp;- phagostimulant based alginate beads:</strong>&nbsp;The entomopathogenic nematode&nbsp;<em>Heterorhabditis indica</em>&nbsp;(IARI strain) is a highly virulent nematode with the ability to kill insect pests belonging to several orders of economic importance. The infective stage juveniles of the nematodes are embedded in sodium alginate beads along with an insect phagostimulant. The bead can either be broadcast in the soil or applied as spot treatment. Laboratory trials have shown the mortality of&nbsp;<em>Spodoptera litura</em>,&nbsp;<em>Helicoverpa</em>&nbsp;spp.,&nbsp;<em>Holotrichia</em>&nbsp;spp., within 48 hours. The shelf-life of the beads is 6-8 months</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Neem seed treatment for nematode management</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\">Control of Ear-cockle and tundu disease of wheat.</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Plant Pathology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Diagnostic kit for graft transmissible pathogens in citrus:&nbsp;</strong>A cost effective and user friendly diagnostic kit for graft transmissible pathogens in citrus has been developed which includes an inexpensive simplified unified nucleic acid (NA) template preparation for both RNA and DNA pathogens and uniform PCR conditions. Uniform PCR conditions coupled with unified NA template preparation will not only reduce the cost of detection but also reduce the time and energy particularly in view of the fact that citrus is hosts of multiple infections of graft transmissible pathogens. The kit also provides information on important viruses and greening bacterium affecting citrus in India</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"text-align:justify\"><strong>Biocontrol Technology for Crop Growth Promotion and Disease Management:</strong>&nbsp;Biological management is an important alternative supporting system of crop protection in which microorganisms have been widely exploited for managing fungal, bacterial, viral and nematodal disease. This technology is an important component of Integrated Pest Management and Organic Farming System. Fungal bioagents&nbsp;<em>Aspergillus niger&nbsp;</em>and&nbsp;<em>Trichoderma harzianum</em>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<em>Trichoderma viridae</em>&nbsp;have been utilized to develop bio-formulations.</p>','plant-protection',1,'2025-06-12 06:19:41','2025-06-13 08:14:44'),(105,'technologies developed','technologies developed',0,'<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" style=\"width:770.333px\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td><img alt=\"\" src=\"http://demo81.eganet.go.tz/tari/uploads/text-editor/images/13_1675347370.jpg\" style=\"height:200px; width:300px\" /></td>\r\n			<td style=\"width:459px\">Hydrophilic polymers have been prepared by grafting synthetic monomers on starch, a naturally occuring polysaccharide polymer for improving the water absorption and delaying its loss. Some polymers based on carboxymethyl cellulose developed recently have shown nearly 33000% m/m water absorption. These hydrogels can be used in diverse areas of agriculture such as dryland/rainfed agriculture, hi-tech horticulture and floriculture, nursery raising in soil less media, soil reclamation, agroforestry, artificial lawns and landscapes, terrace gardening etc. Synthetic pesticides, neem emulsifiable concentrate (EW), Azadirachtin-A concentrate, neem oil microemulsion/microemulsion forming concentrate and processes of making dihydroazadirachtin-A concentrate under ambient conditions have been developed</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Biochemistry</strong></p>\r\n\r\n			<p><strong>Improvement of shelf life and nutritional quality of soya oil</strong> Soya oil contains 20-30 percent oleic acid and 50-55 percent linoleic acid. To increase these heel life and nutritional quality of soya oil content of poly-unsaturated fatty acid, linoleic acid may be decreased and simultaneously the content of mono-unsaturated fatty acid (MUFA), oleic acid may be increased. To achieve this fad 2-1gene has been isolated from soybean seed and characterized. The sense and antisense constructs are being prepared in suitable vector which can further be used to develop transgenic soybean with reduced linoleic acid. <strong>Isolation and characterization of genes involved in lipid metabolism for biosynthesis of designed oil</strong><br />\r\n			Plants store energy in the seeds in the form of triacylglycerol.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td style=\"width:459px\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"http://demo81.eganet.go.tz/tari/uploads/text-editor/images/13_1675347370.jpg\" style=\"height:233px; width:350px\" /></td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>&nbsp;</td>\r\n			<td style=\"width:459px\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>','<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"http://demo81.eganet.go.tz/tari/uploads/text-editor/images/13_1675347370.jpg\" style=\"float:left; height:300px; width:450px\" />Hydrophilic polymers have been prepared by grafting synthetic monomers on starch, a naturally occuring polysaccharide polymer for improving the water absorption and delaying its loss. Some polymers based on carboxymethyl cellulose developed recently have shown nearly 33000% m/m water absorption. These hydrogels can be used in diverse areas of agriculture such as dryland/rainfed agriculture, hi-tech horticulture and floriculture, nursery raising in soil less media, soil reclamation, agroforestry, artificial lawns and landscapes, terrace gardening etc. Synthetic pesticides, neem emulsifiable concentrate (EW), Azadirachtin-A concentrate, neem oil microemulsion/microemulsion forming concentrate and processes of making dihydroazadirachtin-A concentrate under ambient conditions have been developed</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>','technologies-developed',1,'2025-06-12 06:36:15','2025-06-12 07:31:48'),(106,'centres','centres',1,'<p>CENTERS</p>','<p>CENTERS</p>','centers',1,'2025-06-12 07:41:36','2025-06-15 14:42:42'),(107,'Mission and Vision','Mission and Vision',1,'<p>🌟 <strong>Vision</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>To be the <strong>Institute of Excellence</strong> for agricultural research in Tanzania and beyond.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🚀 <strong>Mission</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>To <strong>generate and promote the application of knowledge, innovations, and agricultural technologies</strong> as catalysts for change&mdash;driving agricultural productivity, food and nutrition security, sustainable farming systems, and economic growth. TARI works in close collaboration with national and international stakeholders to ensure inclusive and impactful research outcomes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>💡 <strong>Our Goal</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>To contribute to <strong>increased agricultural productivity</strong> by developing and deploying improved agricultural knowledge and technologies through an <strong>innovation systems approach</strong>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🤝 <strong>Core Values</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>At TARI, our work is guided by a strong foundation of values that shape our culture and commitment to excellence:</p>','<p><!--StartFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🌟 <strong>Vision</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>To be the <strong>Institute of Excellence</strong> for agricultural research in Tanzania and beyond.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🚀 <strong>Mission</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>To <strong>generate and promote the application of knowledge, innovations, and agricultural technologies</strong> as catalysts for change&mdash;driving agricultural productivity, food and nutrition security, sustainable farming systems, and economic growth. TARI works in close collaboration with national and international stakeholders to ensure inclusive and impactful research outcomes.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>💡 <strong>Our Goal</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>To contribute to <strong>increased agricultural productivity</strong> by developing and deploying improved agricultural knowledge and technologies through an <strong>innovation systems approach</strong>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🤝 <strong>Core Values</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>At TARI, our work is guided by a strong foundation of values that shape our culture and commitment to excellence:</p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--EndFragment --></p>','mission-and-vision',1,'2025-06-12 07:50:52','2025-06-28 04:11:33'),(108,'What we Do','What we Do',1,'<p>At the <strong>Tanzania Agricultural Research Institute (TARI)</strong>, we are dedicated to transforming agriculture through science, innovation, and collaboration. Our work spans the entire agricultural value chain&mdash;from research and development to technology dissemination and farmer support&mdash;ensuring that our efforts lead to real-world impact.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🔬 <strong>Research &amp; Innovation</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>We conduct cutting-edge research to develop <strong>technologies, innovations, and management practices (TIMPs)</strong> that address the evolving needs of farmers, agribusinesses, and policymakers. Our research focuses on:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>Crop improvement and breeding for higher yields and resilience</li>\r\n	<li>Soil fertility and sustainable land management</li>\r\n	<li>Integrated pest and disease management</li>\r\n	<li>Climate-smart agriculture and environmental sustainability</li>\r\n	<li>Agricultural mechanization and digital technologies<!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DStartFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p><!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DEndFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🌱 <strong>Technology Development &amp; Dissemination</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI plays a central role in translating research into practice. We develop and promote <strong>farmer-friendly technologies</strong> that enhance productivity, reduce risks, and improve livelihoods. Through field demonstrations, training sessions, and partnerships with extension services, we ensure that innovations reach the people who need them most.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🤝 <strong>Stakeholder Engagement</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>We work closely with a wide range of stakeholders&mdash;including farmers, researchers, extension agents, private sector actors, NGOs, and international partners&mdash;to co-create solutions and foster inclusive agricultural development. Our participatory approach ensures that research is <strong>demand-driven and locally relevant</strong>.<!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DStartFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DEndFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🧭 <strong>Policy Support &amp; Advisory Services</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI provides evidence-based recommendations to inform national agricultural policies and strategies. Our data and insights help shape decisions that promote food security, economic growth, and environmental resilience.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🏢 <strong>National Research Coordination</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>As the coordinating body for the <strong>National Agricultural Research System (NARS)</strong>, TARI oversees and harmonizes research efforts across public and private institutions in Tanzania. We ensure that research activities are aligned with national priorities and contribute to the broader goals of sustainable development.</p>','<p><!--StartFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>At the <strong>Tanzania Agricultural Research Institute (TARI)</strong>, we are dedicated to transforming agriculture through science, innovation, and collaboration. Our work spans the entire agricultural value chain&mdash;from research and development to technology dissemination and farmer support&mdash;ensuring that our efforts lead to real-world impact.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🔬 <strong>Research &amp; Innovation</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>We conduct cutting-edge research to develop <strong>technologies, innovations, and management practices (TIMPs)</strong> that address the evolving needs of farmers, agribusinesses, and policymakers. Our research focuses on:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>Crop improvement and breeding for higher yields and resilience</li>\r\n	<li>Soil fertility and sustainable land management</li>\r\n	<li>Integrated pest and disease management</li>\r\n	<li>Climate-smart agriculture and environmental sustainability</li>\r\n	<li>Agricultural mechanization and digital technologies<!--StartFragment --></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p><!--EndFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🌱 <strong>Technology Development &amp; Dissemination</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI plays a central role in translating research into practice. We develop and promote <strong>farmer-friendly technologies</strong> that enhance productivity, reduce risks, and improve livelihoods. Through field demonstrations, training sessions, and partnerships with extension services, we ensure that innovations reach the people who need them most.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🤝 <strong>Stakeholder Engagement</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>We work closely with a wide range of stakeholders&mdash;including farmers, researchers, extension agents, private sector actors, NGOs, and international partners&mdash;to co-create solutions and foster inclusive agricultural development. Our participatory approach ensures that research is <strong>demand-driven and locally relevant</strong>.<!--StartFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--EndFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🧭 <strong>Policy Support &amp; Advisory Services</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI provides evidence-based recommendations to inform national agricultural policies and strategies. Our data and insights help shape decisions that promote food security, economic growth, and environmental resilience.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🏢 <strong>National Research Coordination</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>As the coordinating body for the <strong>National Agricultural Research System (NARS)</strong>, TARI oversees and harmonizes research efforts across public and private institutions in Tanzania. We ensure that research activities are aligned with national priorities and contribute to the broader goals of sustainable development.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--EndFragment --></p>','what-we-do',1,'2025-06-12 07:54:06','2025-06-28 04:14:43'),(109,'Organization Structure','Organization Structure',1,'<p>The <strong>Tanzania Agricultural Research Institute (TARI)</strong> is structured to ensure efficient coordination, accountability, and delivery of its national research mandate. The organizational framework supports both centralized leadership and decentralized operations across the country.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🏛️ <strong>Governance and Oversight</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>At the apex of the structure is the <strong>Board of Directors</strong>, which provides strategic oversight, policy direction, and institutional governance. The Board ensures that TARI&rsquo;s operations align with national agricultural priorities and uphold standards of excellence and transparency.<!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DStartFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DEndFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></p>\r\n\r\n<p>👤<strong> Executive Leadership</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI is led by the <strong>Director General</strong>, who is responsible for the overall management and administration of the Institute. The Director General oversees the implementation of research programs, institutional development, and stakeholder engagement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🧪 Research Centres and Sub-Centres</p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI operates through a network of <strong>9 main research centres</strong> and <strong>8 sub-centres</strong>, strategically located across Tanzania to address region-specific agricultural challenges.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>Main Centres</strong> are led by <strong>Centre Directors</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>Sub-Centres</strong> are led by <strong>Centre Managers</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>This decentralized model enhances responsiveness, relevance, and farmer engagement across diverse agro-ecological zones.<!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DStartFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DEndFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🧭 <strong>Functional Structure</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI&rsquo;s operations are organized into three main <strong>Departments</strong>, supported by specialized <strong>Units</strong> that provide technical and administrative services:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>📚 <strong>Departments</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li><strong>Research and Innovation</strong><br />\r\n	Oversees the planning, execution, and evaluation of agricultural research programs across all centres.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Technology Transfer and Partnerships</strong><br />\r\n	Facilitates the dissemination of research outputs, farmer training, and collaboration with stakeholders.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Administration and Human Resource Management (HRM)</strong><br />\r\n	Manages institutional support services, staffing, and organizational development<!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DStartFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>🛠️ <strong>Support Units</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<table>\r\n	<thead>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<th>\r\n			<p>Unit</p>\r\n			</th>\r\n			<th>\r\n			<p>Key Responsibilities</p>\r\n			</th>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</thead>\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Planning, Monitoring &amp; Evaluation&nbsp;and Statistics (PME)</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Coordinates the preparation, implementation, and evaluation of institutional plans, budgets and statistical analysis. Prepares budget speeches, annual economic reports, performance assessments, and integrates TARI&rsquo;s plans into national budgeting processes.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Finance and Accounts</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Manages financial operations, including payments, revenue collection, financial reporting, and audit coordination. Ensures compliance with financial regulations.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Internal Audit</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Provides advisory services on resource management, conducts audits, ensures compliance with financial procedures, and evaluates development project performance.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Procurement Management</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Oversees procurement, storage, and supply of goods and services. Ensures adherence to the Public Procurement Act, develops procurement plans, and supports logistical needs.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>ICT </strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Provides ICT support . Implements e-Government policies, maintains software and hardware, manages LAN/WAN communication, and promotes ICT for improved service delivery.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Legal Services</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Offers legal expertise on contracts, agreements, and legislation. Drafts legal documents, liaises with government legal bodies, and manages litigation and court decisions.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>','<p><!--StartFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The <strong>Tanzania Agricultural Research Institute (TARI)</strong> is structured to ensure efficient coordination, accountability, and delivery of its national research mandate. The organizational framework supports both centralized leadership and decentralized operations across the country.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🏛️ <strong>Governance and Oversight</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>At the apex of the structure is the <strong>Board of Directors</strong>, which provides strategic oversight, policy direction, and institutional governance. The Board ensures that TARI&rsquo;s operations align with national agricultural priorities and uphold standards of excellence and transparency.<!--StartFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--EndFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>👤<strong> Executive Leadership</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI is led by the <strong>Director General</strong>, who is responsible for the overall management and administration of the Institute. The Director General oversees the implementation of research programs, institutional development, and stakeholder engagement.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🧪 Research Centres and Sub-Centres</p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI operates through a network of <strong>9 main research centres</strong> and <strong>8 sub-centres</strong>, strategically located across Tanzania to address region-specific agricultural challenges.</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>Main Centres</strong> are led by <strong>Centre Directors</strong></li>\r\n	<li><strong>Sub-Centres</strong> are led by <strong>Centre Managers</strong></li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>This decentralized model enhances responsiveness, relevance, and farmer engagement across diverse agro-ecological zones.<!--StartFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--EndFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🧭 <strong>Functional Structure</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>TARI&rsquo;s operations are organized into three main <strong>Departments</strong>, supported by specialized <strong>Units</strong> that provide technical and administrative services:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>📚 <strong>Departments</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n	<li><strong>Research and Innovation</strong><br />\r\n	Oversees the planning, execution, and evaluation of agricultural research programs across all centres.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Technology Transfer and Partnerships</strong><br />\r\n	Facilitates the dissemination of research outputs, farmer training, and collaboration with stakeholders.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Administration and Human Resource Management (HRM)</strong><br />\r\n	Manages institutional support services, staffing, and organizational development<!--StartFragment --></li>\r\n</ol>\r\n\r\n<p>🛠️ <strong>Support Units</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<table>\r\n	<thead>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<th>\r\n			<p>Unit</p>\r\n			</th>\r\n			<th>\r\n			<p>Key Responsibilities</p>\r\n			</th>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</thead>\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Planning, Monitoring &amp; Evaluation&nbsp;and Statistics (PME)</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Coordinates the preparation, implementation, and evaluation of institutional plans, budgets and statistical analysis. Prepares budget speeches, annual economic reports, performance assessments, and integrates TARI&rsquo;s plans into national budgeting processes.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Finance and Accounts</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Manages financial operations, including payments, revenue collection, financial reporting, and audit coordination. Ensures compliance with financial regulations.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Internal Audit</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Provides advisory services on resource management, conducts audits, ensures compliance with financial procedures, and evaluates development project performance.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Procurement Management</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Oversees procurement, storage, and supply of goods and services. Ensures adherence to the Public Procurement Act, develops procurement plans, and supports logistical needs.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>ICT </strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Provides ICT support . Implements e-Government policies, maintains software and hardware, manages LAN/WAN communication, and promotes ICT for improved service delivery.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p><strong>Legal Services</strong></p>\r\n			</td>\r\n			<td>\r\n			<p>Offers legal expertise on contracts, agreements, and legislation. Drafts legal documents, liaises with government legal bodies, and manages litigation and court decisions.</p>\r\n			</td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--EndFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p><!--EndFragment --></p>','organization-structure',1,'2025-06-12 07:54:34','2025-06-28 04:36:15'),(111,'Utafiti na Ubunifu','Research and Innovation',1,'<p>Research and Innovation</p>','<p>Research and Innovation</p>','research-and-innovation-2',1,'2025-06-13 07:27:53','2025-06-13 07:27:53'),(112,'Uhaulishaji wa Teknolojia na Mahusiano','Technology Transfer and Partnerships',1,'<p>Technology Transfer and Partnerships</p>','<p>Technology Transfer and Partnerships</p>','technology-transfer-and-partnerships',1,'2025-06-13 07:28:32','2025-06-13 07:28:32'),(113,'Administration and HRM','Administration and HRM',1,'<p>The Directorate provide expertise and services on administration and human resources management issues such as recruitment, human resource development and training, promotion, discipline, retention, motivation, performance management and welfare. The Directorate has two sections which are:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>Administration</strong>; Interpret and ensure adherence to staff regulations, Standing Orders and other Labour laws; provide registry, messengerial, courier services and office records management; facilitate employee&rsquo;s relations, welfare (health, sports&nbsp; and culture; handle all protocols; coordinate implementation of Wage Bill for the Institute; coordinate implementation of Business process Improvement and&nbsp; Client Service Charter; coordinate implementation of ethics, value promotion activities, prevention of corrupt practices, diversity issues and private sector participation.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Human Resources Management</strong>; coordinate staff recruitments, selections, placements, confirmations, promotions and transfers for the institute; carryout human resources planning to determine supply and demand for professionals; coordinate implementation of Open Performance Review and Appraisal System (OPRAS), assess the appraisal results; prepare implementation reports; assessment of professional requirements and develop, implement staff development plans;</li>\r\n</ul>','<p>The Directorate provide expertise and services on administration and human resources management issues such as recruitment, human resource development and training, promotion, discipline, retention, motivation, performance management and welfare. The Directorate has two sections which are:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li><strong>Administration</strong>; Interpret and ensure adherence to staff regulations, Standing Orders and other Labour laws; provide registry, messengerial, courier services and office records management; facilitate employee&rsquo;s relations, welfare (health, sports&nbsp; and culture; handle all protocols; coordinate implementation of Wage Bill for the Institute; coordinate implementation of Business process Improvement and&nbsp; Client Service Charter; coordinate implementation of ethics, value promotion activities, prevention of corrupt practices, diversity issues and private sector participation.</li>\r\n	<li><strong>Human Resources Management</strong>; coordinate staff recruitments, selections, placements, confirmations, promotions and transfers for the institute; carryout human resources planning to determine supply and demand for professionals; coordinate implementation of Open Performance Review and Appraisal System (OPRAS), assess the appraisal results; prepare implementation reports; assessment of professional requirements and develop, implement staff development plans;</li>\r\n</ul>','administration-and-hrm',1,'2025-06-13 07:28:51','2025-06-19 10:22:18'),(114,'Planning Monitoring and Evaluation','Planning Monitoring and Evaluation',1,'<p>Planning Monitoring and Evaluation</p>','<p>Planning Monitoring and Evaluation</p>','planning-monitoring-and-evaluation',1,'2025-06-13 07:29:17','2025-06-13 07:29:17'),(115,'Legal Services','Legal Services',1,'<p>Legal Services</p>','<p>Legal Services</p>','legal-services',1,'2025-06-13 07:29:34','2025-06-13 07:29:34'),(116,'Fedha na Uhasibu','Finance and Accounts',1,'<p>Finance and Accounts</p>','<p>Finance and Accounts</p>','finance-and-accounts',1,'2025-06-13 07:29:55','2025-06-13 07:29:55'),(117,'Ukaguzi wa Ndani','Internal Audit',1,'<p>Internal Audit</p>','<p>Internal Audit</p>','internal-audit',1,'2025-06-13 07:31:27','2025-06-13 07:31:27'),(118,'Ugavi','Procurement Management',1,'<p>Ugavi</p>','<p>Ugavi</p>','procurement-management',1,'2025-06-13 07:31:54','2025-06-13 07:31:54'),(119,'Tehama','ICT',1,'<p>ICT</p>','<p>ICT</p>','ict',1,'2025-06-13 07:32:14','2025-06-13 07:32:14'),(120,'TARI UYOLE','TARI UYOLE',1,'<p>TARI UYOLE</p>','<p>TARI UYOLE</p>','tari-uyole',1,'2025-06-13 07:32:50','2025-06-13 07:32:50'),(121,'TARI NALIENDELE','TARI NALIENDELE',1,'<p>TARI NALIENDELE</p>','<p>TARI NALIENDELE</p>','tari-naliendele',1,'2025-06-13 07:33:08','2025-06-13 07:33:08'),(122,'TARI UKIRIGURU','TARI UKIRIGURU',1,'<p>TARI UKIRIGURU</p>','<p>TARI UKIRIGURU</p>','tari-ukiriguru',1,'2025-06-13 07:33:30','2025-06-13 07:33:30'),(123,'TARI KIHINGA','TARI KIHINGA',1,'<p>TARI KIHINGA</p>','<p>TARI KIHINGA</p>','tari-kihinga',1,'2025-06-13 07:33:51','2025-06-13 07:33:51'),(124,'TARI ILONGA','TARI ILONGA',1,'<p>TARI ILONGA</p>','<p>TARI ILONGA</p>','tari-ilonga',1,'2025-06-13 07:34:14','2025-06-13 07:34:14'),(125,'TARI TUMBI','TARI TUMBI',1,'<p>TARI TUMBI</p>','<p>TARI TUMBI</p>','tari-tumbi',1,'2025-06-13 07:34:33','2025-06-13 07:34:33'),(126,'TARI MAKUTUPORA','TARI MAKUTUPORA',1,'<p>TARI MAKUTUPORA</p>','<p>TARI MAKUTUPORA</p>','tari-makutupora',1,'2025-06-13 07:34:57','2025-06-13 07:34:57'),(127,'TARI SELIAN','TARI SELIAN',1,'<p>TARI SELIAN</p>','<p>TARI SELIAN</p>','tari-selian',1,'2025-06-13 07:35:19','2025-06-13 07:35:19'),(128,'TARI MLINGANO','TARI MLINGANO',1,'<p>TARI MLINGANO</p>','<p>TARI MLINGANO</p>','tari-mlingano',1,'2025-06-13 07:35:43','2025-06-13 07:35:43'),(129,'TARI MARUKU','TARI MARUKU',1,'<p>TARI MARUKU</p>','<p>TARI MARUKU</p>','tari-maruku',1,'2025-06-13 07:37:13','2025-06-13 07:37:13'),(130,'TARI TENGERU','TARI TENGERU',1,'<p>TARI TENGERU</p>','<p>TARI TENGERU</p>','tari-tengeru',1,'2025-06-13 07:37:30','2025-06-13 07:37:30'),(131,'TARI KIBAHA','TARI KIBAHA',1,'<p>TARI KIBAHA</p>','<p>TARI KIBAHA</p>','tari-kibaha',1,'2025-06-13 07:37:50','2025-06-13 07:37:50'),(132,'TARI IFAKARA','TARI IFAKARA',1,'<p>TARI IFAKARA</p>','<p>TARI IFAKARA</p>','tari-ifakara',1,'2025-06-13 07:38:13','2025-06-13 07:38:13'),(133,'TARI HOMBOLO','TARI HOMBOLO',1,'<p>TARI HOMBOLO</p>','<p>TARI HOMBOLO</p>','tari-hombolo',1,'2025-06-13 07:38:36','2025-06-13 07:38:36'),(134,'TARI KIFYULILO','TARI KIFYULILO',1,'<p>TARI KIFYULILO</p>','<p>TARI KIFYULILO</p>','tari-kifyulilo',1,'2025-06-13 07:38:58','2025-06-13 07:38:58'),(136,'Cereals','Cereals',1,'<p>Cereals</p>','<p>Cereals</p>','cereals',1,'2025-06-13 07:42:30','2025-06-13 07:42:30'),(137,'Legumes','Legumes',1,'<p>Legumes</p>','<p>Legumes</p>','legumes',1,'2025-06-13 07:43:08','2025-06-13 07:43:08'),(138,'Roots, tubers and Banana (RTB)','Roots, tubers and Banana (RTB)',1,'<p>Roots, tubers and Banana (RTB)</p>','<p>Roots, tubers and Banana (RTB)</p>','roots-tubers-and-banana-rtb',1,'2025-06-13 07:43:24','2025-06-13 07:43:24'),(139,'Horticultural crops','Horticultural crops',1,'<p>Horticultural crops</p>','<p>Horticultural crops</p>','horticultural-crops',1,'2025-06-13 07:43:48','2025-06-13 07:43:48'),(140,'Perennials commercial','Perennials commercial',1,'<p>Perennials commercial&nbsp;</p>','<p>Perennials commercial&nbsp;</p>','perennials-commercial',1,'2025-06-13 07:44:05','2025-06-13 07:44:05'),(141,'Oil crops','Oil crops',1,'<p>Oil crops</p>','<p>Oil crops</p>','oil-crops',1,'2025-06-13 07:44:27','2025-06-13 07:44:27'),(142,'Biotechnology','Biotechnology',1,'<p>Biotechnology</p>','<p>Biotechnology</p>','biotechnology',1,'2025-06-13 07:44:44','2025-06-13 07:44:44'),(143,'Botanicals and Medicinal crops','Botanicals and Medicinal crops',1,'<p>Botanicals and Medicinal crops</p>','<p>Botanicals and Medicinal crops</p>','botanicals-and-medicinal-crops',1,'2025-06-13 07:45:04','2025-06-13 07:45:04'),(144,'Fibre crops','Fibre crops',1,'<p>Fibre crops&nbsp;</p>','<p>Fibre crops&nbsp;</p>','fibre-crops',1,'2025-06-13 07:45:22','2025-06-13 07:45:22'),(145,'Postharvest and management','Postharvest and management',1,'<p>Postharvest and management&nbsp;</p>','<p>Postharvest and management&nbsp;</p>','postharvest-and-management',1,'2025-06-13 07:45:47','2025-06-13 07:45:47'),(146,'Crop Protection','Crop Protection',1,'<p>Crop Protection</p>','<h1><strong>PLANT PROTECTION</strong></h1>\r\n\r\n<p>Hydrophilic polymers have been prepared by grafting synthetic monomers on starch, a naturally occuring polysaccharide polymer for improving the water absorption and delaying its loss. Some polymers based on carboxymethyl cellulose developed recently have shown nearly 33000% m/m water absorption. These hydrogels can be used in diverse areas of agriculture such as dryland/rainfed agriculture, hi-tech horticulture and floriculture, nursery raising in soil less media, soil reclamation, agroforestry, artificial lawns and landscapes, terrace gardening etc. Synthetic pesticides, neem emulsifiable concentrate (EW), Azadirachtin-A concentrate, neem oil microemulsion/microemulsion forming concentrate and processes of making dihydroazadirachtin-A concentrate under ambient conditions have been developed A process for preparing an herbicidal composition comprising of neem leaves extract for the control of Phalaris minor weed without affecting the growth of wheat has also been developed Natural and synthetic polymers, either alone or in combination with inorganic inert have been successfully developed as seed coats. Such coats have been developed employing botanical active ingredients ex. neem meliacins etc. Seed coats based on botanical pesticide will help in improving germination, viability, plantability of a seed or vigour of an agronomic plant that is grown from a treated seed that is planted in a pest infested location, by preventing the fungal/nematode/insect infestation that is a pest for the agronomic plant and against which a meliacin compound has bioactivity Neem oil coated urea (NOCU), Pusaneem GOlden urea and Neem bitter coated urea has been developed. Field efficacy established on rice showed yield increase from 7-17% Dillapoile, an undesirable constituent present on the tune of 20-40% in the essential oil of Anethum sowa ROxb., and its dihydro derivative prepared in laboratory have been developed as indigenous substitutes for the imported and internationally recognized synergist piperonyl butoxide (PBO) Controlled release formulations of phorate have been successfully developed through its monolithic dispersions in polystyrene, polyvinyl chloride, polymethyl-methacrylate, polyethylene glycol and cellulose acetate, ethyl cellulose and starch xanthate. These formulations have been found to provide comparable pest control at one half to one fourth of the phorate content as compared to Thimet 10G, its commercial product A technology fo rmosquito larvicidal preparations based on Rabdosea melissoides has been developed. The essential oil of r. melissoides and its phenolic and non-phenolic constitutents have been developed as potential mosquito larvicidal products. Novel pesticidal oxime esters, alkane diol/polyol mono/di/poly alkanoates, 4-methyl-6-pentyl-a-pyrones have been developed as potent wide spectrum pesticidal products. The compounds are effective particularly as nematicides against Meloidogyne incognita and Rotylenchulus reniformis and fungicides against polyphagous fungi Rhizoctonis solani and Sclerotium rolfsi Cost effective and indigenous know-how for conventional pesticides namely thiophanate-methyl and mancozeb have been developed and passed to NRDC for commercialization.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Entomology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect Pest Management in Cole Crops</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect Pest Management in Okra</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect Pest Management in Brinjal</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect Pest Management in Tomato</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect Pest Management in Cucurbits</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect control in Soybean</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect control in Mustard</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Management of insect pests of Pulses</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect control in Cotton</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Management of insect pests in storage grains</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Insect Pest Management in Rice</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Nematology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The following technologies have been identified for commercialization</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>A novel bio-pesticidal formulation based on an indigenous heat tolerant species of entomopathogenic nematode,<em>&nbsp;Steinernema thermophilum</em>,</strong>&nbsp;incorporated and immobilized into water insoluble superabsorbent hydrogel matrix, has been developed for the first time by a simple method that comprises swelling of the xerogel in appropriate volume of aqueous suspension of the nematode. The formulation obtained has improved shelf life ranging from few hours to 36 months at storage temperatures verying from 5 to 50<sup>0</sup>C. The formulation is not infected by any microorganism and hence does not require any anti-microbial or anti-fungal chemicals</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Application technology for using entomopathogenic nematodes against foliar pest:&nbsp;<strong>Follow spray of 2000 infective juveniles of&nbsp;<em>Steinernema thermophilum</em>&nbsp;per ml liquid formulation, has been found useful for managing diamond backmoth (<em>Plutella xylostella</em>) on cabbage, cotton boll worm (<em>Spodoptera litura</em>) on cotton and whitefly (<em>Bemisia tabaci</em>) on brinjal and tobacco</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><em><strong>Photorhabdus luminescens</strong></em><strong>&nbsp;formulation for insect control</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><em><strong>Heterorhabditis indica</strong></em><strong>&nbsp;- phagostimulant based alginate beads:</strong>&nbsp;The entomopathogenic nematode&nbsp;<em>Heterorhabditis indica</em>&nbsp;(IARI strain) is a highly virulent nematode with the ability to kill insect pests belonging to several orders of economic importance. The infective stage juveniles of the nematodes are embedded in sodium alginate beads along with an insect phagostimulant. The bead can either be broadcast in the soil or applied as spot treatment. Laboratory trials have shown the mortality of&nbsp;<em>Spodoptera litura</em>,&nbsp;<em>Helicoverpa</em>&nbsp;spp.,&nbsp;<em>Holotrichia</em>&nbsp;spp., within 48 hours. The shelf-life of the beads is 6-8 months</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Neem seed treatment for nematode management</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Control of Ear-cockle and tundu disease of wheat.</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Plant Pathology</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Diagnostic kit for graft transmissible pathogens in citrus:&nbsp;</strong>A cost effective and user friendly diagnostic kit for graft transmissible pathogens in citrus has been developed which includes an inexpensive simplified unified nucleic acid (NA) template preparation for both RNA and DNA pathogens and uniform PCR conditions. Uniform PCR conditions coupled with unified NA template preparation will not only reduce the cost of detection but also reduce the time and energy particularly in view of the fact that citrus is hosts of multiple infections of graft transmissible pathogens. The kit also provides information on important viruses and greening bacterium affecting citrus in India</p>\r\n\r\n<p><strong>Biocontrol Technology for Crop Growth Promotion and Disease Management:</strong>&nbsp;Biological management is an important alternative supporting system of crop protection in which microorganisms have been widely exploited for managing fungal, bacterial, viral and nematodal disease. This technology is an important component of Integrated Pest Management and Organic Farming System. Fungal bioagents&nbsp;<em>Aspergillus niger&nbsp;</em>and&nbsp;<em>Trichoderma harzianum</em>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<em>Trichoderma viridae</em>&nbsp;have been utilized to develop bio-formulations.</p>','crop-protection',1,'2025-06-13 07:46:05','2025-06-13 08:14:04'),(147,'Soil Management Research','Soil Management Research',1,'<p>Soil Management Research</p>','<p>Soil Management Research</p>','soil-management-research',1,'2025-06-13 07:46:29','2025-06-13 07:46:29'),(148,'Agroforestry and Climate Change Research','Agroforestry and Climate Change Research',1,'<p>Agroforestry and Climate Change Research</p>','<p>Agroforestry and Climate Change Research</p>','agroforestry-and-climate-change-research',1,'2025-06-13 07:46:51','2025-06-13 07:46:51'),(149,'Agricultural and Irrigation engineering research','Agricultural and Irrigation engineering research',1,'<p>Agricultural and Irrigation engineering research</p>','<p>Agricultural and Irrigation engineering research</p>','agricultural-and-irrigation-engineering-research',1,'2025-06-13 07:47:11','2025-06-13 07:47:11'),(150,'Applied Computing','Applied Computing',1,'<p>Applied Computing</p>','<p>Applied Computing</p>','applied-computing',1,'2025-06-13 07:47:27','2025-06-13 07:47:27'),(151,'Cross cutting issues','Cross cutting issues',1,'<p>Cross cutting issues</p>','<p>Cross cutting issues</p>','cross-cutting-issues',1,'2025-06-13 07:47:44','2025-06-13 07:47:44'),(152,'Adoption and Impact studies','Adoption and Impact studies',1,'<p>Adoption and Impact studies</p>','<p>Adoption and Impact studies</p>','adoption-and-impact-studies',1,'2025-06-13 07:48:06','2025-06-13 07:48:06'),(153,'Value Chain and Marketing','Value Chain and Marketing',1,'<p>Value Chain and Marketing</p>','<p>Value Chain and Marketing</p>','value-chain-and-marketing',1,'2025-06-13 07:48:23','2025-06-13 07:48:23'),(154,'Farming systems','Farming systems',1,'<p>Faming systems</p>','<p>Faming systems</p>','faming-systems',1,'2025-06-13 07:48:39','2025-06-13 08:19:09'),(155,'Internal Program Review (IPR)','Internal Program Review (IPR)',1,'<p>Internal Program Review (IPR)</p>','<p>Internal Program Review (IPR)</p>','internal-program-review-ipr',1,'2025-06-13 07:48:55','2025-06-13 07:48:55'),(156,'Progress Reports','Progress Reports',1,'<p>Progress Reports</p>','<p>Progress Reports</p>','progress-reports',1,'2025-06-13 07:50:39','2025-06-13 07:50:39'),(158,'TARI MIKOCHENI','TARI MIKOCHENI',1,'<p>TARI MIKOCHENI</p>','<p>TARI MIKOCHENI</p>','tari-mikocheni',1,'2025-06-13 08:04:37','2025-06-13 08:04:37'),(160,'varieties-developed','varieties-developed',1,'<p>varieties-developed</p>','<p>varieties-developed</p>','varieties-developed',1,'2025-06-15 14:41:49','2025-06-15 14:41:49'),(161,'research-labs','research-labs',1,'<p>research-labs</p>','<p>research-labs</p>','research-labs-2',1,'2025-06-15 14:43:16','2025-06-15 14:43:16'),(162,'farmers-corner','farmers-corner',1,'<p>farmers-corner</p>','<p>farmers-corner</p>','farmers-corner',1,'2025-06-15 14:43:45','2025-06-15 14:43:45'),(164,'Bodi Ya Wakurugenzi','Board of Directors',1,'<p><!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DStartFragment%20%2D%2D%3E--></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🏛️<strong> Board of Directors</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The <strong>Board of Directors</strong> provides strategic leadership, governance, and oversight for the Tanzania Agricultural Research Institute (TARI). Entrusted with ensuring accountability and institutional excellence, the Board plays a critical role in guiding TARI&rsquo;s mission and long-term vision.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>📌 <strong>Composition</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The TARI Board of Directors is composed of a <strong>Chairperson and 10 members</strong>, representing a diverse range of expertise in agriculture, research, policy, and public service.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🗓️ <strong>Tenure &amp; Meetings</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The inaugural Board was officially inaugurated in <strong>July 2018</strong>, with members appointed to serve a <strong>three-year term</strong>. The Board convenes <strong>quarterly</strong> to:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>Review institutional progress and performance</li>\r\n	<li>Provide strategic direction and policy guidance</li>\r\n	<li>Approve key plans, budgets, and initiatives</li>\r\n	<!--{C}%3C!%2D%2DEndFragment%20%2D%2D%3E-->\r\n</ul>','<p><!--StartFragment --></p>\r\n\r\n<p>🏛️<strong> Board of Directors</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The <strong>Board of Directors</strong> provides strategic leadership, governance, and oversight for the Tanzania Agricultural Research Institute (TARI). Entrusted with ensuring accountability and institutional excellence, the Board plays a critical role in guiding TARI&rsquo;s mission and long-term vision.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>📌 <strong>Composition</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The TARI Board of Directors is composed of a <strong>Chairperson and 10 members</strong>, representing a diverse range of expertise in agriculture, research, policy, and public service.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>🗓️ <strong>Tenure &amp; Meetings</strong></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The inaugural Board was officially inaugurated in <strong>July 2018</strong>, with members appointed to serve a <strong>three-year term</strong>. The Board convenes <strong>quarterly</strong> to:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n	<li>Review institutional progress and performance</li>\r\n	<li>Provide strategic direction and policy guidance</li>\r\n	<li>Approve key plans, budgets, and initiatives</li>\r\n	<!--EndFragment -->\r\n</ul>','board-of-directors',1,'2025-06-28 04:21:38','2025-06-28 04:21:38'),(165,'Seed Catalog','Seed Catalog',0,'<p>DD</p>','<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" style=\"width:1741px\">\r\n	<tbody>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"text-align:center; width:872px\"><strong>SELECT CROP</strong></td>\r\n			<td style=\"text-align:center; width:932px\"><strong>SELECT VARIETY</strong></td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n		<tr>\r\n			<td style=\"width:872px\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"http://demo81.eganet.go.tz/tari/uploads/text-editor/images/SEARCH CROP_1751639519.png\" style=\"height:25px; width:898px\" /></td>\r\n			<td style=\"width:932px\"><img alt=\"\" src=\"http://demo81.eganet.go.tz/tari/uploads/text-editor/images/SEARCH RICE_1751639556.png\" style=\"height:37px; width:904px\" /></td>\r\n		</tr>\r\n	</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"http://demo81.eganet.go.tz/tari/uploads/text-editor/images/details of variety_1751640587.png\" style=\"height:838px; width:1078px\" /></p>','seed-catalog',1,'2025-07-04 11:16:39','2025-07-04 11:49:56');
